{
  "rule_id": "R67_10",
  "target": "ts",
  "tier": "T2",
  "governed_construct": "One module specifier string in a module-loading construct: an import declaration (including type-only and side-effect imports), an export-from re-export declaration, a dynamic import() call, or a require() call whose first argument is a string literal. Code in comments and string literals is not an instance.",
  "interpretation": "The rule governs how modules are referenced. A specifier conforms if it is non-relative: a path alias or base-url path such as '@/utils/date' or 'src/api/products', or a bare package name such as 'react'. A specifier violates if it is relative, i.e. it is '.' or '..' or starts with './' or '../'. Type-only imports/exports, side-effect imports (import './x.css'), re-exports, dynamic import() (even with leading magic comments or split across lines) and require() all count. A file with any relative specifier is VIOLATED; a file with at least one specifier and none relative is SATISFIED; a file with no module-loading construct is NOT_APPLICABLE. Mentions of relative paths inside comments or ordinary string literals are ignored.",
